sentences.t:
La seña sacan fuera, de Valençia dieron salto,
quatro mill menos treínta con mio Çid van a cabo.
a los çinquaenta mill vanlos ferir de grado;

sentences.l:
The banner they-bring out, from Valencia they-gave leap,
four thousand less thirty with my Çid go to end.
to the fifty thousand they-go-them to-strike of liking;

sentences.t:
Mio Çid enpleó la lança, al espada metió mano,
atantos mata de moros que non fueron contados;
por el cobdo ayuso la sangre destellando.

sentences.l:
My Çid employed the lance, to-the sword put hand,
so-many kills of Moors that not they-were counted;
by the elbow downward the blood dripping.

sentences.t:
Al rey Yúcef tres colpes le ovo dados,
saliósle del sol espada, ca muchol andido el cavallo.
metiósle en Gujera, un castiello palaçiano;

sentences.l:
To-the king Yúcef three blows to-him he-had given,
went-out-from-him from-under-the sword, for much-for-him went the horse.
put-himself-from-him in Gujera, a castle palatial;

sentences.t:
Mesnadas de mio Çid robado an el canpo;
entre oro e plata fallaron tres mill marcos,
[de] las otras ganançias non avía recabdo.

sentences.l:
Companies of my Çid plundered have the field;
between gold and silver they-found three thousand marks,
[of] the other gains not there-was reckoning.

sentences.t:
quando al rey de Marruecos assí lo an arrancado,
dexó Álbar Fáñez por saber todo recabdo;
con çient cavalleros a Valençia es entrado,

sentences.l:
when the king of Marruecos thus him they-have routed,
he-left Álbar Fáñez for to-know all reckoning;
with hundred knights to Valencia he-is entered,

sentences.t:
“Ya mugier d[o]ña Ximena, nom lo aviedes rogado?
Estas dueñas que aduxiestes, que vos sirven tanto,
quiérolas casar con de aquestos mios vassallos;

sentences.l:
“Oh wife do[ñ]a Ximena, not-me it had-you asked?
These ladies that you-brought, who you serve so-much,
I-want-them to-marry with of these my vassals;

sentences.t:
Quiérovos dezir lo que es más granado:
non pudieron saber la cuenta de todos los cavallos
que andan arriados e non ha qui tomallos;
los moros de las tierras ganado se an y algo;

sentences.l:
I-want-to-you to-tell that which is more great:
not they-could know the count of all the horses
that go harnessed and not there-is who to-take-them;
the Moors of the lands won themselves have there something;

sentences.t:
El obispo don Jero[me], caboso coronado,
quando es farto de lidiar con amas las sus manos,
non tiene en cuenta los moros que ha matados;
lo que ca[d]ié a él mucho era sobejano;

sentences.l:
The bishop don Jero[me], perfect tonsured-one,
when he-is sated of fighting with both the his hands,
not he-holds in count the Moors that he-has killed;
that which fe[l]l to him much was excessive;
